```c
e = gf_isom_box_array_read(s, bs);
if (!e) {
    ... return GF_OK;
}
if (size<8) return GF_ISOM_INVALID_FILE;

/*hack for some weird files*/
gf_bs_seek(bs, pos);
data = (char*)gf_malloc(sizeof(char) * size);
...
for (i=0; i<size-8; i++) {
    if (GF_4CC(...) == GF_ISOM_BOX_TYPE_ESDS) {
        GF_BitStream *mybs = gf_bs_new(data + i, size - i, GF_BITSTREAM_READ);
        if (ptr->esd) gf_isom_box_del_parent(&ptr->child_boxes, (GF_Box *)ptr->esd);
        ptr->esd = NULL;
        e = gf_isom_box_parse((GF_Box **)&ptr->esd, mybs);
        ...
    }
}
```

So the flow:
1. `gf_isom_box_array_read` parses the child boxes of the mp4a box. This includes the `wave` box which contains an esds subbox. During this, `ptr->esd` gets set to point to the esds inside the wave box. But `gf_isom_box_array_read` returns an error (e.g., because of a malformed box somewhere).
2. We enter the hack path. We scan the raw data for an esds box. We find one (the one inside the wave box, or another one). Since `ptr->esd` is set, we call `gf_isom_box_del_parent(&ptr->child_boxes, ptr->esd)`. This deletes the esd box but leaves the dangling reference in `wave->child_boxes`.
3. Later, during `gf_isom_close`, the wave box is deleted, iterating its child_boxes and dereferencing the freed esd → UAF.

